  - [3mCosts:[0m no history. Once a task is done and gone, there's no record it was ever overdue.
  - [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mB. Persisted store.[0m A [38;5;153mnotifications[39m array in localStorage; the scheduler appends a record ([38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mtaskId[39m, [38;5;153mkind[39m,
  [38;5;153mcreatedAt[39m, [38;5;153mreadAt[39m) when a milestone is crossed.
  - [3mWins:[0m real history and read-state, and a natural place to hang extra delivery channels later.
  - [3mCosts:[0m every task mutation needs reconciliation. Due date moved → the existing overdue record is now a lie. Task
    deleted → orphaned notifications pointing at nothing. Task completed → stale alerts must be swept. That
    reconciliation [3mis[0m the feature's bug surface, and it's most of the work.
  - [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mC. Event log + reducer.[0m Append immutable events, derive views. Correct and auditable, and overkill for one user and
  two milestone types — discarded.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. B's entire extra cost is reconciliation logic that A gets for free by never storing a derived fact, and
  the history B buys has no consumer in a single-user app. If history is ever wanted, A's [38;5;153macknowledged[39m set is a
  superset-friendly upgrade path.

  [1mOne tooling question comes with the design[0m, since this repo has no tooling at all (no package.json, no linter, no
  tests) and it's cheapest to decide before any code exists.
